  {
   "id": "what",
   "h2": "Neuter nouns",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "lead": "Latin nouns are masculine, feminine or <b>neuter</b>. The neuter nouns of the 2nd declension end in <em>-um</em>, and they have one rule worth knowing: the nominative and the accusative are always the same."
    },
    {
     "example": {
      "la": "servus <u class=\"v\">vinum</u> portat.",
      "en": "The slave carries <b>the wine</b>.",
      "note": "<em>vinum</em> is the object here."
     }
    },
    {
     "example": {
      "la": "<u class=\"c\">vinum</u> in taberna est.",
      "en": "<b>The wine</b> is in the shop.",
      "note": "The same word, <em>vinum</em>, is the subject here. Only the sense of the sentence shows which."
     }
    },
    {
     "facts": [
      "A neuter noun has the same ending for the subject and the object: <em>-um</em> in the singular, <em>-a</em> in the plural.",
      "Its other cases go like <em>servus</em>: <em>templi</em>, <em>templo</em>, <em>templorum</em>, <em>templis</em>.",
      "<em>arma</em>, \"weapons\", is only ever plural."
     ]
    },
