  {
   "id": "translate",
   "h2": "Translating them",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "facts": [
      "A neuter noun in <em>-um</em> or <em>-a</em> could be the subject or the object. Find the verb, then ask which makes sense.",
      "An <em>-a</em> ending can be a neuter plural: <em>dona</em> is \"gifts\", <em>templa</em> is \"temples\".",
      "A neuter plural subject still has a plural verb: <em>arma sunt</em>, \"there are weapons\"."
     ]
    },
    {
     "example": {
      "la": "<u class=\"c\">pueri</u> <u class=\"v\">dona</u> portant.",
      "en": "<b>The boys</b> carry <b>the gifts</b>.",
      "note": "<em>dona</em> ends in <em>-a</em>, but it is the neuter plural of <em>donum</em>, and the object."
     }
    },
    {
     "example": {
      "la": "<u class=\"c\">viri</u> <u class=\"v\">arma</u> parant.",
      "en": "<b>The men</b> prepare <b>the weapons</b>."
     }
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "watch",
   "h2": "Watch out",
   "exam": true,
   "label": "Common mistakes",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "facts": [
      "<em>-a</em> on a neuter noun is plural: <em>templa</em> is \"temples\". One temple is <em>templum</em>.",
      "A neuter subject and a neuter object look alike. Use the verb and the sense to decide.",
      "<em>vir</em> is \"man\", and <em>viri</em> can be \"of the man\" or \"the men\".",
      "<em>ager</em> and <em>liber</em> lose their <em>e</em>: <em>agrum</em>, <em>libros</em>."
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
